**user**:
> When I'm applying a PPP conversion to MER data over multiple years, do I use a different conversion rate for each year, or is it like the real unit dollars I'm using, that it's just one?
**assistant**:
> When applying a PPP (Purchasing Power Parity) conversion to MER (Market Exchange Rate) data over multiple years, you generally need to use a different conversion rate for each year. PPP rates are designed to reflect the relative cost of a basket of goods between countries and can vary annually due to changes in inflation, economic conditions, and other factors.
> 
> Each year's PPP conversion rate ensures that the differences in purchasing power and inflation between countries are accurately represented for that specific year. Therefore, applying the correct PPP conversion for each year is akin to adjusting for inflation in real dollar terms, which also requires an annual adjustment.
> 
> If you have access to time series data of PPP conversion factors, it would be best to apply the rate for each specific year to your MER data. This approach will give you a more accurate reflection of long-term economic trends and comparisons.
